   "source": [
    "**WARNING: DATA MISSING FOR OIL AND OCGT + 'Coal PC' emmbed to Lignite & Hard coal**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "508706af",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 2.3.4 - Beyond GHG"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3109bf13",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Systematic approach should always be considered when designing a new (energy) system. Limiting our analysis to GHG is convenient as it enables to compare means of production with on a singular dimension, with a normalized unit: CO2eq.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the environmental transition cannot be restricted to GHG emission reduction. The 9-boundary circle is a relevant representation of the Earth system critical components (https://www.stockholmresilience.org/research/planetary-boundaries.html):\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"images/PB_2025.jpg\" alt=\"Planetary Boundaries\" width=\"500\">\n",
    "\n",
    "Indicators that are usually considered for power generation can be:\n",
    "- Fresh water usage\n",
    "- Land-use\n",
    "- *(Critical)* minerals usage\n",
    "\n",
    "If you want to further investigate these dimensions and compare different technologies, you can look for comparative **Life Cycle Assessements (cLCA)**. They develop normalized quantitative frameworks for different dimensions of the environmental impacts.\n",
    "\n",
    "(A good point of entry: https://www-sciencedirect-com.ezproxy.universite-paris-saclay.fr/science/article/pii/S1364032113005534?via%3Dihub)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "chap3_title",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Chapter 3: Boiteux stacking method (1/2) - only with controllable generation technologies\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "59a0d107",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.1 - Methodology - Sizing a 100% thermal/controllable system with Boiteux stacking\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1be0ed47",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now you hopefully have a better idea of the technologies available, their economic and environmental characteristics.\n",
    "\n",
    "But how to build the most economic-efficient power system, to cover the power demand? This is the question Marcel Boiteux, eminent economist and former CEO of EDF, answered.\n",
    "\n",
    "***NOTEBOOK BREAK- BOITEUX'S STACKING METHOD***"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "db7b9d38",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Questions:**\n",
    "- What is the goal of Boiteux's stacking method?\n",
    "- Why ranging the hours of the year by power demand?\n",
    "- How to get the yearly minimum activation time for a power plant?\n",
    "- How to visually conclude on the capacity to install for each technology of power plant?\n",
    "- What is the impact of the VOLL on the capacity sizing? Can you relate with risk management ? How to ensure an average shortage duration using the VOLL? \n",
    "- What is you decide to have a different VOLL compared to your neighbor?\n",
    "- What are the limits of this methodology?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "aa0153eb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Your answer:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- Find the cheapest mix of power plants that still covers demand (incl. a chosen level of reliability)\n",
    "- Sorting hours by demand (load duration curve) shows directly how many hours each MW of demand is needed → the cost of a plant depends on how long it runs, not on when\n",
    "- Screening curves: annual cost per MW = fixed cost + variable cost × hours → the intersection of two techs gives the break-even hours: h = ΔFixed / ΔVariable (e.g. nuclear vs biomass ≈ 4,760 h)\n",
    "- Mark the break-even hours on the x-axis of the load duration curve → the demand there gives horizontal slices = capacity per technology (base at the bottom, peak at the top)\n",
    "- VOLL = cost of not serving 1 MWh → treated as a \"technology\" with zero fixed cost; a peaker is only built if its fixed cost < VOLL × hours → shortage hours ≈ fixed cost of peaker / (VOLL − its variable cost) ≈ 61,000 / (20,000 − 309) ≈ 3 h/year (= LOLE target). Higher VOLL → more peak capacity, fewer shortage hours (risk vs. cost trade-off)\n",
    "- The country with the lower VOLL builds less peak capacity and relies on its neighbour's plants during scarcity (free-riding); in shared cold spells both are short → needs coordination (e.g. common adequacy standard)\n",
    "- No renewables, storage or interconnection; greenfield (ignores existing plants); no technical constraints (ramping, minimum load, start-ups); loses chronology; one climate year, fixed prices; continuous capacities (real plants come in blocks)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ff1f3e99",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.2 Application to your country"
